---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Project | Trajectory Copilot" description="Project overview, methodology, and success criteria.">
  <section class="space-y-8">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">Trajectory Copilot</h1>

    <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Problem statement</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">As mission design moves deeper into the cislunar environment, trajectory optimization and rendezvous become much more complex. Large language models show strong reasoning and coding ability, while their accuracy and usefulness as engineering copilots for astrodynamics in cislunar space is an area I want to look further into.</p>
        <p class="text-slate-300">The central question is not whether AI can replace astrodynamics tools or mission planners, but rather how can they can help interpret, analyze, and then optimize future trajectories more effectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Why it matters</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">Space operations are expanding into the Earth-Moon system, which demands both technical expertise and extensive computational analysis. If AI can support trajectory design in a trustworthy way, it could improve productivity and, via non-deterministic features, give creative, unique ideas for trajectory planning.</p>
        <p class="text-slate-300">This matters for future cislunar operations, autonomous rendezvous, and space domain awareness workflows where engineers need fast iteration with physically valid results.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Research question</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">Can large language models effectively support spacecraft trajectory design through physics-based mission analysis tools?</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Methodology</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">My workflow combines large language models such as ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, and future frontier systems within the pi harness, coupled with physics-based tools including GMAT and Python astrodynamics libraries.</p>
        <p class="text-slate-300">I hope to test AI-generated mission designs, optimization approaches, and simulation workflows, using the traditional astrodynamics tools to provide physical validation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">System flow</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-col items-center gap-4 md:flex-row md:justify-between">
        <div class="flow-node pulse">Plan</div>
        <div class="text-orbital">→</div>
        <div class="flow-node pulse">Prompt</div>
        <div class="text-orbital">→</div>
        <div class="flow-node pulse">Simulate</div>
        <div class="text-orbital">→</div>
        <div class="flow-node pulse">Validate</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Milestones</h2>
        <ol class="space-y-3 text-slate-300">
          <li><strong>1.</strong> Build a benchmark dataset from known astrodynamics problems.</li>
          <li><strong>2.</strong> Test multiple LLMs on problem setup, explanation, and workflow generation.</li>
          <li><strong>3.</strong> Compare AI-generated solutions against known analytical and numerical results.</li>
          <li><strong>4.</strong> Evaluate whether LLMs can assist an engineer using GMAT or related tools.</li>
          <li><strong>5.</strong> Summarize findings and identify the most promising directions for thesis work.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Success metrics</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300">
          <li>Accuracy of solutions relative to known benchmarks</li>
          <li>Ability to explain and set up optimization problems correctly</li>
          <li>Quality of generated GMAT or simulation workflows</li>
          <li>Consistency across multiple models</li>
          <li>Usefulness to a human engineer working through trajectory design</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Data and restrictions</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">This project uses public data only. The benchmark set is based on non-sensitive reference problems from one of Professor Miller's previous Orbital Dynamics classes, as well as publicly available GMAT resources and examples.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Long-term vision</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">This class project is the foundation for a future master’s thesis on AI-assisted mission planning in more complex dynamic environments, including CR3BP, Earth-Moon trajectory design, cislunar rendezvous, and autonomous mission planning under uncertainty.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
